\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Upset plot illustrating the distribution of exotoxin types and their host targets. This plot is used to visualize the intersections between categories and to highlight class imbalances in the dataset. The upper bar chart shows the intersection sizes, with the tallest bar (approximately 1,694) indicating the most common category intersection. The lower matrix, composed of black-filled circles on a gray background, maps each exotoxin type (Type I, Type II, Type III, Type IV, Unknown) and host target (Vertebrate, Invertebrate) to its intersection, with the horizontal bars indicating the total sequence counts (e.g., Type III with 2,149 sequences, Vertebrate-targeting with 1,863 sequences, and Invertebrate-targeting with 629 sequences).\relax }}{21}{figure.caption.11}}
\newlabel{fig:dataset_distribution}{{4.1}{21}{Upset plot illustrating the distribution of exotoxin types and their host targets. This plot is used to visualize the intersections between categories and to highlight class imbalances in the dataset. The upper bar chart shows the intersection sizes, with the tallest bar (approximately 1,694) indicating the most common category intersection. The lower matrix, composed of black-filled circles on a gray background, maps each exotoxin type (Type I, Type II, Type III, Type IV, Unknown) and host target (Vertebrate, Invertebrate) to its intersection, with the horizontal bars indicating the total sequence counts (e.g., Type III with 2,149 sequences, Vertebrate-targeting with 1,863 sequences, and Invertebrate-targeting with 629 sequences).\relax }{figure.caption.11}{}}
